# Model Component Contracts

## Data-Layer Agnostic Rule

- Treat each dataset output as a generic graph unit (`torch_geometric.data.Data`).
- Do not assume outputs are patches.
- Do not assume outputs are full graphs.
- Samplers must consume whatever graph-unit granularity the data layer emits.

## Required Graph Fields

- `x` must be float tensor of shape `[num_nodes, num_features]`.
- `edge_index` must be `torch.long` tensor of shape `[2, num_edges]`.

Optional fields:

- `edge_attr`, `batch`, `pos`, `graph_y`, `graph_w`, `categorical_index`.

## Encoder Rules

- Prefer native PyG operators over custom message passing.
- Allow only thin adapters around native PyG layers.
- Keep jump-knowledge and pooling behavior explicit and configurable.

## Attention Rules

- MIL attention heads must be optional and config-driven.
- Attention modules return logits; normalization is caller-controlled.

## SSL Rules

- SSL components must be optional and config-driven.
- Disabled SSL must instantiate no SSL modules.

## Loss Rules

- Support categorical, regression, and Cox survival objectives.
- Validate shapes and dtypes at boundaries.
- Avoid hidden broadcasting that can silently mis-train.

## Sampler Rules

- Provide identity and neighborhood strategies.
- Prefer native PyG neighborhood sampler when contracts are satisfied.
- Use custom sampler only when required behavior is unavailable in native path.

## State and Side-Effect Rules

- No mutable global runtime state in model and sampler modules.
- No script-specific hardcoded project logic.
- Constructors must be side-effect free.

## Config-First Constants Rule

- Use named configuration values for hyperparameters and architecture constants.
- Avoid undocumented inline numeric defaults in implementation code.
- Allow small structural literals only when they are shape-level invariants and self-evident.
